---
import reference from "../../../../public/studies/stellar-encounters-2026-11-11/022-reference.json";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find(
  (entry) => entry.slug === "stellar-encounters-2026-11-11",
);
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
  <section>
    <h2>01 / Stopped: the exploration missed the intended breadth</h2>
    <p>
      Closed on 11 November 2026 as stopped. The designer assessed this study as
      a failure to answer the intended design question. Ending it preserves that
      assessment and the limited useful evidence; it does not make the design
      outcome successful. The proposed roster, masses and implementation scope
      were not accepted.
    </p>
    <p>
      The intended subject was distinct stellar natures: how different stars
      look and behave, what opportunities and dangers they create, and how
      colour, surface activity and aura help a pilot recognise them. The
      exploration instead compared a standard Sun with a compact orange dwarf
      and a larger cool giant, concentrating on field geometry and resource
      rates. The designer later described the gap as wanting stars in general,
      “not just three different sizes of sun”.
    </p>
    <p>
      The designer had accepted spatial variation as one interesting ingredient:
      a dwarf could demand precise collection near gravity and a lethal body; a
      giant could provide room to stay and fight. That agreement did not select
      the stellar roster or establish the visual and behavioural identities
      sought by the wider inquiry. The assistant acknowledged treating that
      limited agreement as a broader answer and moving into readiness and scope
      prematurely.
    </p>
    <p>
      Historical question test, 11 November 2026: One subject — Pass; Grounded —
      Pass with findings; Consequence — Pass. It tested the original opening,
      which was corrected without a rerun, and did not establish the study’s
      eventual usefulness or design success.
      <a href="/lab/reviews/stellar-encounters-2026-11-11"
        >Original review and findings</a
      >.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2 id="spatial-identities">
      02.1 / Retained observation: spatial variety can matter
    </h2>
    <p>
      The first example used the production plateau/linear collection law and
      Wasp energy costs to compare local collection, exposure and hull
      clearance. Its dwarf had a 25 u body and collection ending at 180 u; the
      giant had a 110 u body and collection ending at 520 u. These were
      deliberately compressed candidate profiles, not measured stellar
      properties.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <img
        src="/studies/stellar-encounters-2026-11-11/021-fields-160.png"
        alt="Captured radial field comparison of the standard Sun, quiet dwarf and cool giant, with the Wasp centre at 160 units"
        loading="lazy"
      >
      <figcaption>
        Original 02.1 capture, 11 November 2026, 1280 px browser viewport. At
        160 u, coasting consumption is 0.8 E/s. The dwarf provides 1.05 E/s with
        no exposure; the giant provides 11.17 E/s with 2.10 hull/s exposure.
        This is a local field calculation, with no gravitational motion,
        resource limits or enemies.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>
      The designer found the dwarf’s limited manoeuvring space interesting and
      the giant’s broad field illustrative of a different situation. This is a
      retained design judgment about one ingredient, not acceptance of these
      candidates as the answer to stellar variety.
      <a href="/studies/stellar-encounters-2026-11-11/021-reference.json"
        >Original field conditions and readings</a
      >.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2 id="room-under-pull">
      02.2 / Retained check: geometry under one gravity profile
    </h2>
    <p>
      The Motion instrument compared the same Wasp, fittings and burn against
      three body/field profiles, keeping standard-Sun gravity identical. B1
      began at x −400 u relative to the star, offsets +80, +160 and +260 u,
      speed 120 u/s along +x, zero spin, for 14 s at 60 Hz with four substeps.
      There were no planets, enemies, wrapping or randomness. Energy and
      exposure budgets did not alter motion; contact ended a trace.
    </p>
    <table>
      <caption>
        Captured local spans that pay for full forward thrust without exposure
      </caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Candidate</th>
          <th>Centre-distance span</th>
          <th>Width</th>
          <th>Width beyond gravity</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {reference.summaries.map(({ encounter, band }) => (
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">{encounter.name}</th>
            <td>
              {band.inner.toFixed(0)}–{band.outer.toFixed(0)} u
            </td>
            <td>{band.width.toFixed(0)} u</td>
            <td>
              {Math.max(
                0,
                band.outer - Math.max(band.inner, reference.gravity.reach),
              ).toFixed(0)}{" "}
              u
            </td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      In the captured B1 run, the 80 u approach hit the standard Sun but cleared
      the dwarf with about 5.4 u of hull clearance and less than a second of
      collection. The giant’s 160 u approach cleared its body by about 7.5 u
      with an analytical exposure budget of 1.86 hull. Its broad collection
      profile included a region outside the gravity cutoff. These checks put
      numbers to the spatial idea; they did not establish sustained survival,
      score balance or a new stellar character.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <img
        src="/studies/stellar-encounters-2026-11-11/022-dwarf.png"
        alt="Recorded dwarf trajectories and distance, gravity and energy graphs"
        loading="lazy"
      >
      <figcaption>
        Dwarf reference, captured 11 November 2026. Full B1 trajectories, with
        the cursor at 1.5 s; path scale 0.2 px/u centred on the star. The close
        trajectory survives contact but its full 14 s budget spends reserve.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <details>
      <summary>Standard Sun and giant captures, with exact conditions</summary>
      <figure>
        <img
          src="/studies/stellar-encounters-2026-11-11/022-sun.png"
          alt="Recorded standard Sun trajectories, including terminal contact on the close approach"
          loading="lazy"
        >
        <figcaption>
          Standard Sun, same B1 inputs, initial states, camera scale and 1.5 s
          cursor. The close trace stops at contact.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure>
        <img
          src="/studies/stellar-encounters-2026-11-11/022-giant.png"
          alt="Recorded giant trajectories showing the larger body and collection boundary"
          loading="lazy"
        >
        <figcaption>
          Giant, same B1 inputs, initial states, camera scale and 1.5 s cursor.
          The collection boundary extends beyond the dashed gravity cutoff.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        <a href="/studies/stellar-encounters-2026-11-11/022-reference.json"
          >Captured reference configuration, results and source hashes</a
        >. Base revision {reference.baseRevision}, with uncommitted changes. The
        record retains the burn sequence, original game definitions and
        settings. These images show complete recorded trajectories, not
        player-controlled flying or a perceptual motion assessment. No motion
        clip was retained.
      </p>
    </details>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2 id="readiness">
      03 / The intended stellar identities remained unanswered
    </h2>
    <p>
      The designer clarified that examples such as a white giant, a blue dwarf,
      or a star too violent to harvest without damage were prompts for a broader
      inquiry. The missing result was a considered set of different stellar
      natures, connecting scientific basis, pilot experience and visible
      identity. Their names were not a settled taxonomy. This cycle did not
      resolve those characters or how colour, surface behaviour and aura would
      communicate them.
    </p>
    <p>
      The original spatial model remains limited evidence. Final families,
      masses, artwork, activity behaviour, resource-limited flight and combat
      balance are unresolved. The passing question test and automated checks do
      not override the designer’s assessment that this study failed its purpose.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2 id="scope">04 / No accepted implementation scope</h2>
    <p>
      The assistant drafted shared star profiles, simulation/HUD integration and
      a Suns Lab trial, with proposed dwarf/giant mass ratios of 0.7/1.0
      relative to the standard Sun. Those proposals were not accepted and do not
      carry forward as requirements or delivery authorization. The pre-closure
      page is retained in repository history at
      d718d29bfd169bae421410246cbfb168ed6b21bb.
    </p>
    <p>
      The temporary interactive comparisons and their dedicated code were
      retired after checking consumers. Shared production systems and reusable
      instruments remain. This static record preserves the useful observations
      and their limits without requiring historical simulation code to execute.
    </p>
    <p>
      A separate
      <a href="/lab/studies/stellar-characters-2026-11-11"
        >Stellar characters study</a
      >
      already records the broader inquiry. The
      <a href="/lab/reviews/stellar-encounters-2026-11-11"
        >historical question review</a
      >
      remains available. The evidence closure is recorded in
      <code>docs/loop-evidence/2026-11-11-stellar-encounters-closure.md</code>.
      No factory lessons or standing rules were adopted as part of closure.
    </p>
  </section>
</StudyLayout>

<style>
figure {
  margin: var(--spacing-lg) 0;
}
img {
  display: block;
  width: 100%;
  height: auto;
}
figcaption {
  max-width: var(--measure-prose);
  margin-top: var(--spacing-sm);
  color: var(--color-text-muted);
  font-size: var(--font-size-caption);
}
summary {
  color: var(--color-action);
  cursor: pointer;
  padding-block: var(--spacing-md);
}
table {
  width: 100%;
  border-collapse: collapse;
  margin-block: var(--spacing-lg);
  font-size: var(--font-size-label);
}
caption {
  text-align: left;
  color: var(--color-text-muted);
  margin-bottom: var(--spacing-md);
}
th,
td {
  padding: var(--spacing-sm);
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  text-align: left;
  vertical-align: top;
}
code {
  overflow-wrap: anywhere;
}
</style>
